  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <p>Looking for a beach where dogs are welcome in Six-Fours-les-Plages? Brutal Beach sets aside a dedicated area on Bonnegrâce beach, year-round.</p>

      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>At the southern tip of the large Bonnegrâce beach, on the Promenade Charles-de-Gaulle, a stretch of coast goes by the name "Brutal Beach" (linked to its history with surfers) — first known as a surf and windsurf spot, it also welcomes dogs in this marked-off section, which the town hall officially calls "Plage Amie des Animaux" (confirmed by the official ville-six-fours.fr website and a Facebook post from the town hall). The dedicated area is 50 metres long.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Leash compulsory, open access year-round, and category 1 and 2 dogs excluded. Dogs must stay under their owner's control and must not disturb other coastal users, in particular the board-sport practitioners who also frequent this area.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: head to the Presqu'île du Gaou and its shaded trails at the end of the day to extend the outing without the heat.</div>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Are dogs allowed on Bonnegrâce beach?</h3>
      <p>Yes, in a dedicated 50-metre area, officially named "Plage Amie des Animaux" by the town hall.</p>
      <h3>Is a leash compulsory?</h3>
      <p>Yes, leash compulsory, open access year-round.</p>
      <h3>Which dogs are excluded from this area?</h3>
      <p>Category 1 and 2 dogs.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Île du Grand Gaou and its wild trails, nearby, also welcome dogs — a good way to continue the walk after the beach.</p>
    </div>

